Section 3a's own step 3 says "at call sites, apply function summaries (section
4)", and that is the branch where a tainted value is used at a line defining
nothing: it may have been CONSUMED (fmt.Printf(cwd) returns a byte count) or
ESCAPED INTO A RECEIVER (lst.append(x)), and only the first lets the walk close
a branch. load_function_summaries and infer_summary had zero production
callers; the walk now consults the catalogue at exactly that branch.

AN EMPTY CATALOGUE IS A STRICT NO-OP, BY DESIGN. get_default_summary returns
param_to_return={0..9: True} -- everything propagates -- so using it would make
an empty catalogue CHANGE behaviour, every callee reading as passing taint on.
An uncatalogued callee reads as unknown instead, so a catalogue covering
nothing reproduces the pre-section-4 output exactly and every behaviour change
is attributable to an entry somebody wrote. Pinned by its own test.

QUALIFIED NAMES ONLY -- the safety property. load_function_summaries also
indexes every entry under its bare last component; 11 of its 33 keys are such
aliases, including log, map, filter and parse. A short-name match would let
audit.log(secret) resolve to console.log and read as terminating. A false
"terminates" REMOVES A REAL SECURITY FINDING, so the alias index is never
consulted. Every clause of the terminating predicate is a conjunction for the
same asymmetry: a wrong "terminates" costs recall silently, a wrong
"propagates" only leaves an unknown unknown.

THE CATALOGUES ARE ENUMERATION-DERIVED, NOT A STDLIB SURVEY. The two shipped
catalogues were rust and typescript -- the two languages with NO working DDG,
one of them with zero sinks -- while python and go, the only two that have one,
had none. Rather than guess at stdlib breadth, the walk was instrumented on the
cohort to enumerate the callees it actually consults at escape sites: 264
escape sites, 47 distinct names. go_stdlib.yaml and python_stdlib.yaml name all
18 of those a qualified-name catalogue can express; the other 29 are external.*
(callee module unresolved) or first-party in-repo, neither addressable by YAML.

MEASURED EFFECT: 25 OF 264 ESCAPE SITES CLOSE (9.5%), AND ZERO FLOWS CHANGE.
Cohort A/B on the same binary, catalogue live vs forced empty:
  16 confirmed / 40 unknown / 0 refuted -- IDENTICAL in both arms.
Structural, not a coverage shortfall: a walk is unknown if ANY branch escapes,
so a flow becomes refutable only when EVERY escape on it closes. 5 of 37
unknown walks did close a site; every one still had another open. What holds
them open: 81 no-call-at-line, 40 catalogued-but-propagates, 21 unresolved
module, 12 uncatalogued. At the margin -- the 14 walks held open by exactly ONE
site -- the single blocker is "no call at the line" in 10 of 14.

SO THE BINDING CONSTRAINT ON REFUTATION IS NOT THE CATALOGUE. It is that 40.5%
of escape sites are not calls at all: the value went into a field write, a
container subscript or a closure capture. That is ADR-0017 section 7a
field-sensitivity / alias analysis, which section 7b explicitly EXCLUDES.

INV-sadah does NOT close and WI-kabif's removal authority stays blocked -- now
for a reason that has been measured rather than assumed. Its filed list of four
prerequisites was already six; this makes seven.

